<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Exponential Decay</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01320</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Exponential Decay</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c6eb0ba4-0fc5-4b53-a0ba-eb5331b825bc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Every year after a new car is purchased, it loses <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mfrac>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mfrac>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> of its value.
        Another way to think about this is that the car retains <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mfrac>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mfrac>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> of its value
        each year. Let’s say that a new car’s value is $18,000.</para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">1. A buyer worries that the car will be worth nothing in three years.
            Do you agree? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Your answers may vary, but here is an example:</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Disagree. Sample explanation: If the car loses a third of its value
            each year, then after one year its value will be <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of $18,000 or $12,000. After two years its value will be <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of $12,000 or $8,000. And after three years its value will be <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of $8,000 or about $5,333. The buyer might have thought that “losing a third of its
            value each year” meant losing $6,000 each year, so after 3 years the car would be
            worthless (because <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>). He may not realize
            that after one year, the value of the car is no longer $18,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">2. Write an expression to show how to find the value of the car for
            each year listed in the table. </para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
            <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Year</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Value of Car (Dollars)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 0 </entry>
                  <entry> 18,000 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 1 </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 2 </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 3 </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 6 </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
          <para id="para-00006"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00002">
            <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Year</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Value of Car (Dollars)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 0 </entry>
                  <entry> 18,000 </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 1 </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 2 </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                    or <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 3 </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                    or <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry> 6 </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                        <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mfrac>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                          </m:mrow>
                        </m:mfrac>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                        </m:msup>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">3. Write an equation relating the value of the car in dollars, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>v</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, to the number of years, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. </para>
          <para id="para-00009"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>v</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">4. Use your equation to find <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>v</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> when <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is 0. What does this
            value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>v</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> mean in this situation?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00014"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>v</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>18</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. This is the value of
            the car when <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, which is when the car is
            purchased.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">5. A different car loses value at a different rate. The value of this
            different car in dollars, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>d</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, after <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> years can be represented
            by the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>d</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.
            Explain what the numbers 10,000 and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            mean in this situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00017">10,000 is the initial value of the car in dollars, and the car
            retains <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of its value each year.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise></section>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00003"><title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00018">Start with an equilateral triangle with area 1 square unit, divide it into 4 congruent pieces as in the figure,
      and remove the middle one. Then, repeat this process with each of the remaining pieces. Repeat this process over
      and over for the remaining pieces. The figure shows the first two steps of this construction.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">
                  <media alt="Three blue triangles show the stages of forming a Sierpinski triangle: starting with a solid triangle, then removing the center, and finally removing the centers of the remaining triangles, creating a recursive pattern.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_07_03_01.svg"/>
              </media>

            </para>
            <para id="para-00020">What fraction of the area is removed each time? How much area is
              removed after the <m:math display="inline"><m:msup><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mtext>th</m:mtext></m:msup></m:math> step? Use a calculator to find out how much area remains in the
              triangle after 50 such steps have been taken.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00022">Each step, <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              of the remaining area is removed, since taking away <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              from each smaller triangle results in taking <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              of the total remaining area. That means that each step, the remaining area is
              multiplied by <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              since <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>–</m:mo>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              So after <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> steps, the
              remaining area is simply <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:mfrac>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              After 50 steps, this leaves an area of only about 0.000000566321656 square units, so
              there’s almost nothing left!</para>
            <para id="para-00023">If you can imagine doing this process forever, the resulting shape
              is called the Sierpinski triangle.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">
              <media alt="A large blue triangle filled with smaller blue triangles arranged in a repeating pattern, creating a fractal design known as the Sierpinski triangle. The inner triangles are inverted and evenly spaced throughout.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_07_03_02.svg"/>
              </media>

            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00025"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_5_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Analyzing Exponential Decay</title>
        <para id="para-00026">At the beginning of April, a colony of ants had a population of 5,000.</para>
        <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
          <item>The colony decreases by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            during April. Write an expression that represents the ant population at the end of
            April.</item>
          <item>During May, the colony decreases again by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of its size. Write an expression that represents the ant population at the end of May.</item>
          <item>The colony continues to decrease by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of its size each month. Write an expression that represents the ant population after 6
            months.</item>
          <item>Write an equation to represent the population of ants, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>p</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, after <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> months.</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00027">When you are analyzing exponential decay and are given the fraction or
          percent by which it decreases, you need to remember that one minus that fraction or
          percent is the growth rate. In the case of the ant colony, because it is decreasing by <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, that
          means that the growth factor is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>–</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00028">Part 1 asks for an expression to represent the ant population at the
          end of April. There are several options:</para>
        <list id="list-00005">
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
                <m:mo>–</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>4000</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00029">Each line is the simplification of the line above it. Part 2 has a few
          more options.</para>
        <para id="para-00030">Part 2 is asking you to find <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> of
          April’s total. There are several expressions to represent that:</para>
        <list id="list-00006">
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>4000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>4000</m:mn>
                <m:mo>–</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mn>4000</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>3200</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00031">Basically, you take the answer from April and multiply by <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> again
          since the growth factor is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. You
          could also look at it as taking the initial value times <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>
          twice.</para>
        <para id="para-00032">Part 3 asks you to extend the pattern to 6 months after the initial
          population was measured. After the <m:math><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mtext>nd</m:mtext></m:msup></m:math> month, you multiplied the initial value by the
          growth factor twice, so for this situation you would need to multiply the initial value by
          the growth factor 6 times, or raise it to the sixth power. The possible answers would be <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>
          or <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, which
          means that after 6 months the population would be about 1,311.</para>
        <para id="para-00033">Part 4 wants the general equation to represent the situation. If you
          use the pattern from part 3, you can see that it can be written in the exponential form <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, where <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is the initial value and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is the growth factor. Using the
          variables suggested, the equation would be <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>p</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5000</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Analyzing Exponential Decay</title>
    <para id="para-00034">A doctor
          prescribes 125 milligrams of a therapeutic drug that decreases in effectiveness by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
          each hour.</para>
    <exercise id="exer-00007">
            <problem id="prob-00007">

              <para id="para-00035">a. Write an expression to represent how much is left after 1 hour.</para>
              <para id="para-00036">b. Write an expression to represent how much is left after 3 hours.</para>
              <para id="para-00037">c. Write an equation that represents the amount of drug, <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>d</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, left after <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> hours.</para>
              <para id="para-00038"/>

            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00007">
              <para id="para-00039">Compare your answers:</para>

              <para id="para-00040">Here is how to analyze the exponential decay situation:</para>

              <para id="para-00041">In part a, you need to multiply the initial amount by <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          or <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>125</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          or 87.5 mg.</para>

              <para id="para-00042">In part b, since it’s 3 hours later, it would be <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>125</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          or <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>125</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          or 42.875 mg.</para>

              <para id="para-00043">The equation that represents the situation for part c is <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>d</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>125</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>

            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
</document>